<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-pipeline-trace">
  <title>Field-by-Field Pipeline Trace</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      This chapter follows five small synthetic responses through the current
      deterministic short-answer baseline. The fixtures are designed to make each stage
      result predictable. They are not student data, research findings, or the frozen
      <c>CS1</c>–<c>CS10</c> codebook.
    </p>

    <p>
      The trace uses a synthetic vocabulary containing <c>pivot_in_every_col</c>,
      <c>system_consistent</c>, <c>free_var_exists</c>, and
      <c>no_unique_solution</c>. Its synthetic codebook contains three keyword rules:
      <c>pivot_in_every_col</c> maps to <c>TRACE-CS-PIVOT</c>,
      <c>free_var_exists</c> maps to <c>TRACE-CS-FREE</c>, and
      <c>no_unique_solution</c> maps to <c>TRACE-CS-NOT-UNIQUE</c>. These artificial
      identifiers prevent the trace from implying claims about the governing research
      codebook.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-trace-cases">
    <title>Trace Fixtures</title>

    <table xml:id="tab-trace-fixtures">
      <title>Synthetic responses used in the trace.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="12%"/>
        <col width="48%"/>
        <col width="40%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell><p>ID</p></cell><cell><p>Response text</p></cell><cell><p>Case exercised</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>T1</p></cell><cell><p><c>system_consistent because pivot_in_every_col</c></p></cell><cell><p>Canonical tokens and one matching code.</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>T2</p></cell><cell><p><c>no_unique_solution because free_var_exists</c></p></cell><cell><p>Canonical tokens and two distinct candidate codes.</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>T3</p></cell><cell><p>A unique solution is impossible because there is a free variable.</p></cell><cell><p>Ordinary student wording that the literal vocabulary baseline does not normalize.</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>T4</p></cell><cell><p><c>[1 0 0 0 | *]</c></p></cell><cell><p>Mathematical work without a verbal connector.</p></cell></row>
        <row bottom="minor"><cell><p>T5</p></cell><cell><p>No.</p></cell><cell><p>Bare answer without justification.</p></cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      All five fixtures use one synthetic question, import, vocabulary version, codebook
      version, and pipeline run. That shared provenance lets the stage tables be read as
      one coherent execution.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-trace-ingest">
    <title>Stage 1: Ingest and Derive</title>

    <p>
      Core ingest registers the source file and writes generic events. Short-answer
      derive then creates one <c>responses</c> row per eligible event. The following table
      suppresses unrelated event fields but preserves the identifiers needed downstream.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-trace-stage1">
      <title>Selected <c>responses</c> fields after derive.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="13%"/><col width="13%"/><col width="20%"/><col width="54%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell><p><c>response_id</c></p></cell><cell><p><c>import_id</c></p></cell><cell><p><c>question_id</c></p></cell><cell><p><c>response_text</c></p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>trace-t1</p></cell><cell><p>1</p></cell><cell><p>trace-question</p></cell><cell><p><c>system_consistent because pivot_in_every_col</c></p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>trace-t2</p></cell><cell><p>1</p></cell><cell><p>trace-question</p></cell><cell><p><c>no_unique_solution because free_var_exists</c></p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>trace-t3</p></cell><cell><p>1</p></cell><cell><p>trace-question</p></cell><cell><p>A unique solution is impossible because there is a free variable.</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>trace-t4</p></cell><cell><p>1</p></cell><cell><p>trace-question</p></cell><cell><p><c>[1 0 0 0 | *]</c></p></cell></row>
        <row bottom="minor"><cell><p>trace-t5</p></cell><cell><p>1</p></cell><cell><p>trace-question</p></cell><cell><p>No.</p></cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      The visible identifiers above are abbreviated for readability. In a real database,
      response identifiers are stable hash-derived text values, and the response rows also
      retain student, timestamp, and course information.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-trace-screen">
    <title>Stage 2: Screen</title>

    <p>
      The rule screener looks only at response text. It excludes blanks, no-effort text,
      bare yes/no answers, and some very short unsupported responses. It includes a
      response with a recognized connector. Mathematical work without a connector is
      flagged for review.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-trace-stage2">
      <title>Selected <c>screenings</c> fields.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="14%"/><col width="12%"/><col width="16%"/><col width="16%"/><col width="42%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell><p><c>response_id</c></p></cell><cell><p><c>run_id</c></p></cell><cell><p><c>label</c></p></cell><cell><p><c>confidence</c></p></cell><cell><p><c>rationale</c></p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>trace-t1</p></cell><cell><p>8</p></cell><cell><p><c>INCLUDE</c></p></cell><cell><p>0.85</p></cell><cell><p>Contains a connector linking observation to conclusion.</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>trace-t2</p></cell><cell><p>8</p></cell><cell><p><c>INCLUDE</c></p></cell><cell><p>0.85</p></cell><cell><p>Contains a connector linking observation to conclusion.</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>trace-t3</p></cell><cell><p>8</p></cell><cell><p><c>INCLUDE</c></p></cell><cell><p>0.85</p></cell><cell><p>Contains a connector linking observation to conclusion.</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>trace-t4</p></cell><cell><p>8</p></cell><cell><p><c>FLAG</c></p></cell><cell><p>0.55</p></cell><cell><p>Mathematical work is present, but no connector links it to a conclusion.</p></cell></row>
        <row bottom="minor"><cell><p>trace-t5</p></cell><cell><p>8</p></cell><cell><p><c>EXCLUDE</c></p></cell><cell><p>0.90</p></cell><cell><p>Bare yes/no answer with no verbal justification.</p></cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      Confidence is a heuristic score, not a calibrated probability. Extraction receives
      only the three <c>INCLUDE</c> rows. T4 and T5 remain in the database and later receive
      an escalation disposition; they are not deleted.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-trace-extract">
    <title>Stage 3: Extract</title>

    <p>
      The keyword extractor locates the first supported connector. In a sentence of the
      form <q><m>X</m> because <m>Y</m></q>, the right clause becomes the legacy
      <c>premise</c> and the left clause becomes the legacy <c>conclusion</c>. It then
      searches each clause for a literal canonical-form vocabulary token. The longest
      canonical token found in a clause is kept. A clause with no literal token is stored
      as cleaned raw text and marks the step OOV.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-trace-stage3-chains">
      <title>Selected <c>chains</c> fields.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="15%"/><col width="15%"/><col width="15%"/><col width="55%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell><p><c>chain_id</c></p></cell><cell><p><c>response_id</c></p></cell><cell><p><c>run_id</c></p></cell><cell><p><c>extractor_notes</c></p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>101</p></cell><cell><p>trace-t1</p></cell><cell><p>8</p></cell><cell><p><c>connector=because</c></p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>102</p></cell><cell><p>trace-t2</p></cell><cell><p>8</p></cell><cell><p><c>connector=because</c></p></cell></row>
        <row bottom="minor"><cell><p>103</p></cell><cell><p>trace-t3</p></cell><cell><p>8</p></cell><cell><p><c>connector=because</c></p></cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <table xml:id="tab-trace-stage3-steps">
      <title>Selected <c>chain_steps</c> fields.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="12%"/><col width="12%"/><col width="28%"/><col width="34%"/><col width="14%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell><p><c>chain_id</c></p></cell><cell><p><c>step_index</c></p></cell><cell><p><c>premise</c></p></cell><cell><p><c>conclusion</c></p></cell><cell><p><c>oov_flag</c></p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>101</p></cell><cell><p>1</p></cell><cell><p><c>pivot_in_every_col</c></p></cell><cell><p><c>system_consistent</c></p></cell><cell><p>0</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>102</p></cell><cell><p>1</p></cell><cell><p><c>free_var_exists</c></p></cell><cell><p><c>no_unique_solution</c></p></cell><cell><p>0</p></cell></row>
        <row bottom="minor"><cell><p>103</p></cell><cell><p>1</p></cell><cell><p>there is a free variable</p></cell><cell><p>A unique solution is impossible</p></cell><cell><p>1</p></cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      T3 demonstrates a deliberate baseline limitation. A human recognizes the phrase
      <q>there is a free variable</q>, but the baseline vocabulary matcher searches for the
      literal token <c>free_var_exists</c>. The raw phrase is preserved and OOV is set
      rather than silently normalizing the paraphrase. The current extractor produces at
      most one step and does not store a separate inferential-link text or link-status
      field.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-trace-match">
    <title>Stage 4: Match</title>

    <p>
      The keyword matcher compares the stored premise and conclusion with accepted codebook
      keyword patterns and semicolon-delimited surface markers. It records one row per
      distinct code, with every firing rationale joined into one explanation. Structural
      <c>slot=term</c> patterns do not fire in the current baseline.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-trace-stage4">
      <title>Selected <c>matches</c> fields for the synthetic codebook.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="13%"/><col width="13%"/><col width="22%"/><col width="16%"/><col width="36%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell><p><c>match_id</c></p></cell><cell><p><c>step_id</c></p></cell><cell><p><c>code_id</c></p></cell><cell><p><c>version_id</c></p></cell><cell><p><c>rationale</c></p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>201</p></cell><cell><p>1001</p></cell><cell><p><c>TRACE-CS-PIVOT</c></p></cell><cell><p>3</p></cell><cell><p>Keyword pattern <c>pivot_in_every_col</c> matched on the premise.</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>202</p></cell><cell><p>1002</p></cell><cell><p><c>TRACE-CS-FREE</c></p></cell><cell><p>3</p></cell><cell><p>Keyword pattern <c>free_var_exists</c> matched on the premise.</p></cell></row>
        <row bottom="minor"><cell><p>203</p></cell><cell><p>1002</p></cell><cell><p><c>TRACE-CS-NOT-UNIQUE</c></p></cell><cell><p>3</p></cell><cell><p>Keyword pattern <c>no_unique_solution</c> matched on the conclusion.</p></cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      T3 produces no match in this synthetic setup because neither raw clause contains a
      synthetic keyword pattern. No row is written to represent <q>zero matches</q>; that
      absence is interpreted by the disposer. Candidate-code presence does not establish
      that a code is conceptually correct, nor do the null correctness fields establish an
      incorrect criterion or link. They simply have not been scored.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-trace-dispose">
    <title>Stage 5: Dispose</title>

    <p>
      The rule disposer reads the screening label, chain presence and size, OOV count,
      and distinct candidate codes. The first applicable rule wins.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-trace-stage5">
      <title>Selected <c>dispositions</c> fields.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="14%"/><col width="12%"/><col width="24%"/><col width="50%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell><p><c>response_id</c></p></cell><cell><p><c>run_id</c></p></cell><cell><p><c>disposition</c></p></cell><cell><p><c>trigger_note</c></p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>trace-t1</p></cell><cell><p>8</p></cell><cell><p><c>auto_accept</c></p></cell><cell><p>Single clean match: <c>TRACE-CS-PIVOT</c>.</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>trace-t2</p></cell><cell><p>8</p></cell><cell><p><c>standard_review</c></p></cell><cell><p>Two distinct codes: <c>TRACE-CS-FREE</c> and <c>TRACE-CS-NOT-UNIQUE</c>.</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>trace-t3</p></cell><cell><p>8</p></cell><cell><p><c>terminology_flag</c></p></cell><cell><p>One out-of-vocabulary step.</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>trace-t4</p></cell><cell><p>8</p></cell><cell><p><c>escalate</c></p></cell><cell><p><c>FLAG</c> at Stage 2.</p></cell></row>
        <row bottom="minor"><cell><p>trace-t5</p></cell><cell><p>8</p></cell><cell><p><c>escalate</c></p></cell><cell><p><c>EXCLUDE</c> at Stage 2.</p></cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      <c>auto_accept</c> means only that the current cascade found one clean candidate.
      It does not turn the tool proposal into a final research code. T4 and T5 illustrate
      why human review is a standing layer rather than a sixth computational stage: a
      person may confirm exclusion, recover a codable episode from work, or leave the case
      unresolved without changing the five-stage architecture.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-trace-human-review">
    <title>Human Review</title>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-trace-review-actions">
      <title>Review Actions</title>

    <p>
      The following decisions illustrate how a reviewer might use the stored evidence.
      They are examples, not results from an implemented review page.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-trace-review">
      <title>Illustrative review decisions.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="10%"/><col width="20%"/><col width="25%"/><col width="45%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell><p>ID</p></cell><cell><p>Tool route</p></cell><cell><p>Reviewer action</p></cell><cell><p>Reason</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>T1</p></cell><cell><p><c>auto_accept</c></p></cell><cell><p>Confirm <c>TRACE-CS-PIVOT</c>.</p></cell><cell><p>The synthetic question frame defines the pivot criterion as the relevant control and the trace contains no competing evidence.</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>T2</p></cell><cell><p><c>standard_review</c></p></cell><cell><p>Replace the two-code candidate set with <c>TRACE-CS-FREE</c>.</p></cell><cell><p>The second hit came from a judgment token, not a distinct criterion-to-judgment control. The reviewer records why the keyword hit was a false alternate.</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>T3</p></cell><cell><p><c>terminology_flag</c></p></cell><cell><p>Map the phrase to <c>free_var_exists</c>, then add the appropriate code.</p></cell><cell><p>The wording is an attested paraphrase of an existing vocabulary proposition, not evidence for a new control.</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>T4</p></cell><cell><p><c>escalate</c></p></cell><cell><p>Leave unresolved pending question context.</p></cell><cell><p>Work is visible, but no judgment or inferential connection can be recovered from the fixture alone.</p></cell></row>
        <row bottom="minor"><cell><p>T5</p></cell><cell><p><c>escalate</c></p></cell><cell><p>Confirm that no episode is codable.</p></cell><cell><p>The response supplies a judgment but no identifiable criterion.</p></cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      For T2 and T3, the reviewer should preserve the original tool-code list, the final
      reviewer-code list, and a rationale in the override log. T4's unresolved state belongs
      in review status and notes because unresolved is not an override edit type. Local
      correctness and goal coverage would be assessed from the actual question frame; this
      synthetic trace does not invent those values.
    </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-trace-final-record">
      <title>Final Auditable Record</title>

    <p>
      A final record is not one flattened label. It is a connected set of records that lets
      another analyst reconstruct what the tool proposed and what the reviewer changed.
      For T2, the audit path is:
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-trace-final-record">
      <title>Illustrative final record for T2.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="24%"/><col width="76%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell><p>Record</p></cell><cell><p>Preserved value</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>Source</p></cell><cell><p>Import 1, response <c>trace-t2</c>, synthetic question <c>trace-question</c>.</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>Run provenance</p></cell><cell><p>Run 8, deterministic baseline implementation labels, synthetic vocabulary version 2, synthetic codebook version 3.</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>Screening</p></cell><cell><p><c>INCLUDE</c>, confidence 0.85, connector rationale.</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>Extraction</p></cell><cell><p>One ordered step: <c>free_var_exists</c> to <c>no_unique_solution</c>; OOV 0; connector <c>because</c>.</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>Tool candidates</p></cell><cell><p><c>TRACE-CS-FREE</c> and <c>TRACE-CS-NOT-UNIQUE</c>, each with its firing rationale.</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>Tool route</p></cell><cell><p><c>standard_review</c>; two distinct candidates.</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>Review</p></cell><cell><p>Completed by a named reviewer against run 8 and the stated question frame.</p></cell></row>
        <row bottom="minor"><cell><p>Override</p></cell><cell><p><c>replace</c>; tool list contains both candidates; reviewer list contains <c>TRACE-CS-FREE</c>; rationale explains that the second hit names a judgment rather than a separate control.</p></cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      This structure preserves uncertainty and correction as data. It also keeps a future
      reanalysis possible: a new matcher can create a new run without rewriting run 8, and
      the earlier reviewer decision remains interpretable against the versions that were
      active when it was made.
    </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
</chapter>
